;; waydroid_appfuse -- let vold's AppFuse mount succeed on a Fedora host.
;;
;; WHY THIS EXISTS
;;
;; StorageManager.openProxyFileDescriptor() is how an Android app serves a real
;; file descriptor for bytes it has no file for. vold does the work: it mounts a
;; FUSE filesystem at /mnt/appfuse/<uid>_<mountId> and the app answers the
;; protocol. Every openDocument in a synthesising DocumentsProvider -- cloud
;; clients, archive browsers, encrypted vaults -- goes through it. Without this
;; module every one of them fails, on every call:
;;
;;   app :  IllegalStateException: Failed to mount
;;   vold:  Failed to mount /mnt/appfuse/<uid>_<id>: Invalid argument
;;
;; FUSE is not the problem. /dev/fuse is present, vold runs, persist.sys.fuse is
;; true, and three FUSE mounts are already live for emulated storage. The problem
;; is the mount options, read out of this host's own copy of the image at
;; /var/lib/waydroid/rootfs/system/bin/vold:
;;
;;   fd=%i,rootmode=40000,default_permissions,allow_other,user_id=0,group_id=0,
;;   context="u:object_r:app_fuse_file:s0",fscontext=u:object_r:app_fusefs:s0
;;
;; A MAINLINE Waydroid runs against the HOST's SELinux policy, not Android's.
;; Fedora's policy cannot parse either context, so mount(2) returns EINVAL before
;; FUSE is ever reached.
;;
;; WHERE THE KERNEL SAYS SO -- AND WHERE IT DOES NOT
;;
;; `ausearch` is COMPLETELY CLEAN for this. A context that cannot be parsed never
;; becomes an AVC, so the audit subsystem has nothing to report and the obvious
;; first move gives the wrong answer. The kernel does say it, in dmesg:
;;
;;   SELinux: security_context_str_to_sid (u:object_r:app_fuse_file:s0)
;;            failed with errno=-22
;;
;; -22 is EINVAL. Look in dmesg, not ausearch.
;;
;; THREE IDENTIFIERS ARE MISSING, NOT ONE
;;
;; Measured on bigtab01 2026-09-24 with security_check_context():
;;
;;   u:object_r:app_fuse_file:s0         INVALID   <- what vold asks for
;;   system_u:object_r:app_fuse_file:s0  INVALID   <- so the TYPE is unknown
;;   u:object_r:fusefs_t:s0              INVALID   <- and so is the USER `u`,
;;                                                    with a type Fedora has
;;   system_u:object_r:fusefs_t:s0       VALID     <- control: the probe is sound
;;
;; Declaring the two types alone would still have returned EINVAL. The SELinux
;; *user* `u` has to exist too. Fedora ships system_u, unconfined_u, staff_u,
;; sysadm_u, guest_u and root; Android's entire policy uses the single letter `u`,
;; and nothing on a Fedora host has ever needed it.
;;
;; WHY DECLARING AN SELINUX USER IS SAFE
;;
;; A user in an object context is only ever a name to be looked up. The kernel's
;; policydb_context_isvalid() special-cases the object_r role: for object contexts
;; it checks that user, role and type all *exist* and that the MLS level is valid,
;; and it does NOT check that the user is authorised for the role. So `u` needs no
;; roles, no privileges and no login mapping, and no process will ever run as it --
;; only inodes on this one mount carry it. It grants nothing by existing.
;;
;; EVERY LINE BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING, PROVEN BY REMOVAL
;;
;; Verified on bigtab01 2026-09-24 with appfuse-probe against this container.
;; Each variant was loaded with `semodule -i` and the probe re-run:
;;
;;   this module, as it stands          AppFuse works, 7/7 correctness, 8/8 fds
;;   minus (typeattributeset file_type) IOException: FuseUnavailableMountException
;;                                        -- a DIFFERENT symptom; the mount now
;;                                        succeeds and the app cannot use it
;;   minus the (associate) rule         back to IllegalStateException: Failed to mount
;;   minus nothing else                 -- see the next note
;;
;; AND WHAT WAS TRIED AND PROVED UNNECESSARY, so nobody adds it back:
;;
;;   allow container_runtime_t app_fusefs (filesystem (mount unmount remount
;;                                        getattr relabelto associate))
;;   allow container_runtime_t app_fuse_file (file (relabelto))
;;   allow container_runtime_t unlabeled_t (filesystem (relabelfrom))
;;   allow container_runtime_t fusefs_t (filesystem (relabelfrom))
;;   ...plus a wide dir/file access set for container_runtime_t and unconfined_t
;;
;; All of it is redundant. container_runtime_t already holds those permissions
;; through the base policy's attribute-based rules, which is exactly why the two
;; typeattributeset lines matter so much: assigning `file_type` and
;; `filesystem_type` is what makes the existing rules apply to these new types.
;; Declaring the types WITHOUT the attributes gets a mount nothing can read.
;; That is the whole trick -- the attribute does the work an explicit rule set
;; would only have duplicated, less safely.
;;
;; WHY PRIVATE TYPES AND NOT AN ALIAS OF fusefs_t
;;
;; Aliasing app_fuse_file to fusefs_t would also have worked and would have been
;; two lines. It would also have inherited every fusefs_t rule in the base policy,
;; host-wide, for a mount the container controls. Private types keep the grant
;; where it belongs -- the same argument waydroid_backlight.cil makes at length.
;;
;; Written in CIL rather than .te for the same reason as that module: semodule
;; compiles CIL directly, so this needs no selinux-policy-devel, which on an
;; rpm-ostree host would cost a layered install and a reboot.
;;
;; See docs/55-appfuse.md. Verify with bin/appfuse-test.sh.

;; The SELinux user Android uses and Fedora does not have. A name, not a
;; privilege -- see above. s0 is the only level vold ever asks for.
(user u)
(userrole u object_r)
(userlevel u (s0))
(userrange u ((s0)(s0)))

;; The label vold puts on every inode of the mount, via context=.
;; The file_type line is what lets the container read its own mount.
(type app_fuse_file)
(roletype object_r app_fuse_file)
(typeattributeset file_type (app_fuse_file))

;; The label vold puts on the superblock, via fscontext=.
(type app_fusefs)
(roletype object_r app_fusefs)
(typeattributeset filesystem_type (app_fusefs))

;; The inode label has to be allowed to live on that filesystem or the mount is
;; refused outright -- the same associate permission waydroid_backlight_t needs
;; on sysfs_t. This is the only allow rule the module needs.
(allow app_fuse_file app_fusefs (filesystem (associate)))
